Backend Analysis: Retail ERP (Node/Express/TS/Mongoose)
1. Auth & authorization

backend/src/utils/auth.ts:20-45 — Password hashing is custom crypto.scrypt with random 16-byte salt, 64-byte key, stored as salt:hash hex, compared with crypto.timingSafeEqual. This is actually a reasonable scheme (scrypt is memory-hard, salted, timing-safe compare) — better than the README's dismissive framing suggests, though it uses Node's default scrypt cost parameters (N=16384) rather than tuned ones, and there's no documented rationale/versioning if params ever need to change. Severity: low.
backend/src/utils/auth.ts:47-76 — JWT is hand-rolled (HMAC-SHA256 sign/verify, manual base64url), not the jsonwebtoken library (confirmed absent from package.json). Functionally equivalent to HS256 JWT but untested against edge cases (alg confusion isn't possible since only HMAC is implemented, which is good, but there's no jti/revocation list, so tokens can't be invalidated before expiry — e.g., deactivating a user via isActive:false only blocks future requests because requireAuth re-fetches the user each time, so that part is fine). Severity: low-medium (custom crypto in security-critical code is generally discouraged even when correct).
backend/src/utils/auth.ts:18 — JWT_SECRET defaults to hardcoded string 'change-me-in-production' if env var missing; if ops forgets to set it in Vercel, all tokens become forgeable with a public default. Severity: high.
backend/src/routes/index.ts:31 — Global requireAuth applied to all /api/* routes except /auth/*, then per-route authorizeRoles — role checks look consistent across products/categories/purchases (owner-only writes) and sales/payments/deliveries/expenses/stock-adjustments (owner+staff). No route found bypassing this.
backend/src/routes/index.ts:52-58 (payments, deliveries) — routes are gated by authorizeRoles('owner','staff') but the controllers (paymentsController.ts, deliveriesController.ts) themselves take plain Request (not AuthenticatedRequest) and never record createdBy, so there's no audit trail of who created/modified a payment or delivery, unlike Sale/Purchase/Expense/StockAdjustment. Severity: low.
backend/src/controllers/authController.ts:21-27 — First registered user always becomes owner (bootstrap pattern); after that, registration requires an authenticated owner. Reasonable, but no rate-limiting on /login (see below) enables brute-force against the derived scrypt hash.
2. Input validation

No validation library anywhere (zod/joi/express-validator absent from package.json). All validation is ad-hoc inline if checks in controllers, inconsistent in depth.
backend/src/controllers/productsController.ts:90-109 (createProduct) — doesn't validate price/costPrice are numbers or non-negative before Product.create (Mongoose schema min:0 will catch it, but status, images, expiryDate etc. from req.body are passed through unchecked via spread ...req.body).
backend/src/controllers/paymentsController.ts:14, 19 and deliveriesController.ts:44 — Payment.create(req.body) / findByIdAndUpdate(req.params.id, req.body) pass the raw body straight to Mongoose with no allow-list, letting a caller set arbitrary schema fields (e.g. paymentStatus directly to completed on creation, bypassing any intended workflow). Severity: medium.
backend/src/controllers/productsController.ts:167 and deliveriesController.ts:44 — findByIdAndUpdate(req.params.id, req.body, {runValidators:true}) — mass-assignment: any field in the schema (including createdBy) can be overwritten by whoever has owner role, no field whitelist. Severity: low-medium.
req.params.id is passed directly into Model.findById everywhere (e.g. salesController.ts:125, productsController.ts:84) without ObjectId format validation — an invalid id throws a Mongoose CastError, which is uncaught in several non-asyncHandler-wrapped paths... actually all routes are wrapped via asyncHandler, so it lands in the global error handler and returns 500 with err.message (see #3) rather than a clean 400. Severity: low.
3. Security issues

backend/src/app.ts:53-56 — Global error handler does res.status(500).json({ error: err.message }), leaking internal error messages (e.g. Mongoose CastError text, DB errors) to clients; no stack trace suppression concerns since stack isn't sent, but message leakage is still present. No NODE_ENV check to hide details in production. Severity: medium.
No helmet (no security headers: no CSP, no X-Content-Type-Options, etc.) — confirmed absent from dependencies. Severity: medium.
No rate limiting anywhere (/api/auth/login, /api/auth/register fully exposed to brute force). Severity: high.
CORS (backend/src/app.ts:13-44): reflects credentials: true with an origin-checking callback; requests with no Origin header are always allowed (app.ts:19), which is fine for server-to-server/curl but combined with credentials:true isn't itself exploitable by browsers (browsers always send Origin for cross-site fetch). Preview-domain matching (app.ts:29-33) uses origin.endsWith(domain) — this allows any hostname ending in .pages.dev claimed suffix, e.g. evil-product-management-c0p.pages.dev would NOT match since it checks suffix not prefix, so this is reasonably safe, but note it doesn't require the . before the domain, so notproduct-management-c0p.pages.dev (no dot) would still match by endsWith. Minor, severity: low.
No NoSQL-injection sanitization (no express-mongo-sanitize). Most filters build queries from typed/coerced values (String(...), Number(...)), which limits injection surface, but productsController.ts:56 (filter.category = category) and :57 (createdBy) pass req.query values directly as ObjectId filter values without casting — Mongoose will reject non-ObjectId strings via CastError (caught by global handler → 500), not truly exploitable for injection but a robustness gap. Severity: low.
Real .env is properly gitignored and not committed (verified via git ls-files). No hardcoded secrets found in source.
4. Data/model issues

backend/src/models/Payment.ts and Delivery.ts have no productId→referential FK check enforced at schema level (delivery does check existence in controller); Payment model has no link to Sale at all — a payment isn't tied to any order/sale record beyond a free-text orderId: String field, so there's no referential integrity between Sales and Payments. Severity: medium.
backend/src/models/Category.ts:19 — unique compound index {name:1, parent:1} prevents duplicate category names under the same parent, but doesn't prevent circular parent references (a category could be set as its own ancestor via chained parent updates — no cycle check anywhere in categoriesController.ts). Severity: low.
backend/src/models/Product.ts has no index on category or status despite getProducts/getLowStockProducts filtering on both frequently; only a text index exists. Severity: low-medium (perf, not correctness).
backend/src/models/Purchase.ts and StockAdjustment.ts have no index on createdAt/productId despite being queried/sorted by these. Severity: low.
Money stored as plain Number (float) everywhere (price, costPrice, amount, grandTotal, etc.) rather than integer minor-units or Decimal128 — floating-point summation in JS (salesController.ts:43-72) can accumulate rounding error over many line items. Severity: medium.
5. Business logic correctness

Stock updates for Sales/Purchases/StockAdjustments/Deliveries all correctly use mongoose.startSession() + session.startTransaction() wrapping the read-modify-write of product.stock (e.g. salesController.ts:20-106), which prevents lost-update races provided the deployment MongoDB is a replica set/Atlas (transactions require it) — confirmed .env.example:3 shows a plain mongodb://localhost:27017/... standalone URI with no replica-set params, so local dev likely can't run transactions at all (startSession/startTransaction throw on standalone Mongo). Severity: high (deployment/dev-env correctness risk, not a logic bug).
salesController.ts:37-38 — stock decrement done inside the loop per item without pre-checking all items' availability first; if item 3 of 5 fails, items 1-2 already had .save() called within the transaction, but that's fine since the whole transaction aborts — correct behavior, just worth noting it's not optimized (N sequential awaits instead of bulk).
COGS/profit math (salesController.ts:45-73, analyticsController.ts) is internally consistent: itemProfit = itemRevenue - itemCogs, aggregated correctly, margins computed guard against divide-by-zero. No correctness bug found.
analyticsController.ts:12-16 — expense date-range reuses the sale's createdAt range object directly as expenseDate range (expenseMatch.expenseDate = match.createdAt), which works today only because both filters use the same {$gte,$lte} shape — fragile coupling, not a bug currently.
dashboardController.ts computes totalRevenue from Payment collection (paymentStatus:'completed'), but Sales revenue is tracked independently in Sale.grossRevenue — these two revenue figures are unrelated/never reconciled, so the dashboard's "total revenue" likely doesn't match actual POS sales. Severity: medium (business-logic mismatch, confusing metric).
6. Error handling

Centralized handler exists (app.ts:53-56) and all routes are wrapped in asyncHandler (routes/index.ts:23-26), so uncaught async errors are consistently funneled to it. Status codes are used sensibly (400 for validation, 401/403 for auth, 404 for not-found, 500 fallback). Reasonably consistent overall — one of the stronger parts of the codebase.
7. Code quality

Heavy duplication of pagination boilerplate (page/limit clamping + Promise.all([find, countDocuments])) copy-pasted across salesController.ts:109-116, purchasesController.ts:65-77, stockAdjustmentsController.ts:60-72, expensesController.ts:31-57, productsController.ts:40-71 — no shared pagination helper.
authController.ts:6, purchasesController normalizedItems, and several spots use any liberally, weakening type safety despite strict:true in tsconfig.json.
src/middleware/.gitkeep, src/services/.gitkeep, src/utils/.gitkeep — empty placeholder folders (services/ unused entirely — no service layer, all logic lives in controllers).
src/index.ts is a vestigial re-export ("Legacy entrypoint kept for compatibility") — dead/confusing entry point alongside server.ts and api/index.ts.
8. Tests

No test files (*.test.ts/*.spec.ts) and no Jest/Vitest config or dependency anywhere in the repo. Zero test coverage. Severity: high.
9. Deployment sanity

backend/src/config/db.ts:3-18 — Connection caching uses a module-level variable (cachedConnection), which works within a warm Vercel Lambda instance but is the standard pattern; however api/index.ts:11 calls connectDb on every invocation before delegating to app(req,res) — fine given the cache guard, but there's no connection-readiness/error differentiation from a cold connect timeout, and no bufferCommands:false/serverless-specific Mongoose tuning, which can cause hanging requests during connection storms on scale-to-zero. Severity: low-medium.
As noted in #5, transactions used throughout require a replica-set MongoDB; if deployed against a non-Atlas standalone instance, every Sale/Purchase/StockAdjustment/Delivery write will throw. Severity: high if misconfigured.
10. Scripts

backend/src/scripts/seed.ts:26-28 and createUser.ts:12-14 — hardcoded demo credentials admin@example.com / password123 (owner role). If a developer runs seed.ts against a production database (no env guard/confirmation prompt), this creates a real owner account with a trivial, publicly-visible-in-source password. No check for NODE_ENV !== 'production' before seeding. Severity: high.
