Routing (App.tsx)
frontend/src/App.tsx:16-29 — normalizePath uses an if/else chain against pathname only; any unmapped path (typo, deep link, or a genuinely unknown route) silently falls through to /dashboard instead of a 404. No true not-found handling. Medium
App.tsx:42-47 — navigate() no-ops if nextRoute === route, meaning clicking the same nav item does nothing even if query params changed — but since query string isn't part of AppRoute/state, deep links like /products?edit=<id> (built in BarcodeScannerPage.tsx:190-196) work only via full pushState + manual popstate dispatch, bypassing navigate() entirely (see BarcodeScannerPage.tsx:193-194,201-203,209-211,216-217). Two different mechanisms drive the same routing state — footgun for future maintainers. Medium
App.tsx:36-40 — Back/forward via popstate is handled, but the query string is dropped by normalizePath (it only reads pathname), so ProductManagementPage/PurchasesPage/StockAdjustmentsPage each independently re-parse window.location.search in their own useEffect (ProductManagementPage.tsx:76-78, PurchasesPage.tsx:49-50, StockAdjustmentsPage.tsx:33-34) — duplicated logic, and it only runs once on mount, so navigating from Scanner→Products while already on /products won't re-trigger the edit/prefill (no route effectively "changes"). Medium
App.tsx:49-63 — Two separate useEffects do redirect-to-login and redirect-to-dashboard by directly mutating history.replaceState; this is a workable but fragile hand-rolled guard — no centralized "protected route" abstraction, so every new admin-only page needs its own inline user.role === 'owner' check duplicated in both the nav (App.tsx:112-126) and the render switch (App.tsx:149-151).
Auth & state
frontend/src/api/api.ts:9 — JWT stored in localStorage (pm_auth_token), read on every request via interceptor. Classic XSS-exfiltration risk since anything that can inject a script can read localStorage directly (vs httpOnly cookie). High (severity elevated because there's no CSP evidenced and axios withCredentials: true alongside bearer-token-in-localStorage is a mixed pattern).
No response interceptor exists anywhere (confirmed via grep — zero matches for response.use/401 handling in src/). A 401 from an expired/invalid token is caught individually by each page's own try/catch and shown as a generic error string (e.g. ProductManagementPage.tsx:64, SalesPage.tsx:95) rather than triggering logout+redirect to /login. Only AuthContext.tsx:37-45's initial /auth/me bootstrap clears the token on failure; a token that expires mid-session leaves the user stuck seeing "Failed to load..." errors on every page instead of being redirected to login. High
Role gating — Consistent for nav/route level (App.tsx:112-126,149-151, all owner-only pages gate correctly). No stray admin buttons found inside staff-visible pages (Expenses/Stock Adjustments have no owner-only actions embedded). Gating relies entirely on the frontend since there's no evidence of a shared "requireRole" component — it's copy-pasted user.role === 'owner' conditionals.
API layer
Centralized client (api/api.ts + trivial re-export api/client.ts:1-3, which appears redundant/dead — both exist and only api.ts is imported elsewhere; client.ts seems unused). Low
Error handling is duplicated per-call: virtually every page repeats requestError?.response?.data?.error || requestError?.message || 'fallback' (e.g. CategoriesPage.tsx:35, PurchasesPage.tsx:37,56,120, ExpensesPage.tsx:29,75, StockAdjustmentsPage.tsx:40,76, SalesHistoryPage.tsx:44,220). No shared getErrorMessage(err) helper despite the exact same expression appearing 15+ times. Medium (maintainability, not a bug)
